  "checklist": [
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Supply (VDD) must be 3.75 to 6 V, and the datasheet recommends VREG + 0.3 V up to 6 V: 4.5 V or more for this 4.20 V part. The absolute maximum is 7.0 V.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 11,
        "where": "Sec. 3.1; DC characteristics and Absolute Maximum Ratings, p. 3"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "This is the -2 option: it charges to 4.20 V (+/-0.75%). Use it only with cells that charge to 4.20 V. The -3, -4 and -5 options regulate to 4.35, 4.40 and 4.50 V.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 3,
        "where": "Voltage regulation, VREG"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Set the charge current with RPROG: IREG (mA) = 1000 / RPROG (kOhm). 2 kOhm gives 500 mA. RPROG must be 2 to 67 kOhm, and the datasheet prefers a 1C charge rate, so 500 mA suits a cell of about 500 mAh or more.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 15,
        "where": "Sec. 5.1.2; RPROG range p. 4; Sec. 6.1.1.1 p. 17"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Fit at least 4.7 uF from VDD to ground and at least 4.7 uF from VBAT to ground. The VBAT capacitor keeps the loop stable when the battery is disconnected.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 11,
        "where": "Secs. 3.1 and 3.2"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "This is a linear charger, so the voltage drop is dissipated as heat. From 5 V into a 3.6 V cell at 500 mA that is about 0.7 W. The SOT-23-5 package is rated 230 C/W (4-layer test board), so the chip would overheat; it then lowers the charge current by thermal regulation. Expect slower charging on a small board, and give the package copper.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 5,
        "where": "Thermal resistance, p. 5; thermal regulation, p. 1 (the power is calculated here)"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "recommended",
      "text": "If the input is hot-pluggable, as a USB cable is, add input overvoltage protection: the datasheet suggests a transient suppressor from the supply connector to ground.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 18,
        "where": "Sec. 6.1.1.2"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "STAT is low while charging, high when charging is complete and high impedance when there is no battery or the charger is shut down (MCP73831). The LED between VBUS and STAT through 470 ohm lights while charging. STAT can also drive a microcontroller input.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 15,
        "where": "Table 5-1; Sec. 3.3 p. 11"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "Letting PROG float, or driving it high, disables the charger and ends the charge cycle. A switch or transistor on PROG can inhibit charging.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 18,
        "where": "Sec. 6.1.1.6"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "recommended",
      "text": "The charger does not protect the cell from over-discharge or a short circuit. Use a cell with a protection circuit, or add one.",
      "basis": "convention"
    }
  ],
